@startuml proto-dht11-timing
' ===========================================================================
' proto-dht11-timing.puml - (c) the DHT11 handshake, in the numbers both sides
' actually produce.
'
' Measured by tb/tb_pe_soc_dht11.v on real RTL (60 MHz, Icarus, the real SRAM
' macro) with firmware/dht11_read.pe:
'
'   start signal: line low for 18093.0 us          (floor 18 000 us)
'   host-high window: 30.0 us, then released on pin_oe   (band 20..40 us)
'   40 samples, 40 bits, bytes 2c 01 aa 55 2c
'   sample margin: 17.0 us past the longest 0-release, 25.0 us before the 1 ends
'   bit-transitions in the frame: 24 of 39
' ===========================================================================

title DHT11 - handshake timing\n<font size=12>18 093 us start; 30.0 us host window; sample 45 us into the release, margins 17.0 / 25.0 us</font>

hide stereotype
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam sequence {
  ArrowColor #33555
  LifeLineBorderColor #33555
  LifeLineBackgroundColor #FAFAFA
  ParticipantBorderColor #33555
  ParticipantBackgroundColor #F4F7FB
  ParticipantFontName monospaced
  ActorFontName monospaced
  NoteFontName monospaced
  GroupFontName monospaced
}

participant "CPU\npe_cpu.v" as CPU
participant "pad 6\nTXPIN / PINOE" as PAD
participant "the wire\n+ pull-up" as WIRE
participant "DHT11 model\nworst case of\neach window" as SENSOR
participant "the 45 us\nsample" as SMP

== 1. the start signal: the host resets the sensor ==

CPU -> PAD : PINOE = 0x00, TXPIN = DHT_DATA, PINOE = DHT_DATA
note right of PAD
  The idle state is the pull-up. Open-drain is
  deliberately NOT set: the 30 us high below is a
  DRIVEN high, not a release.
end note
CPU -> PAD : TXPIN = 0x00 - the line goes low
note over CPU
  LONG pair (n2,n3) = (6,255), n1 = 212
  one outer step = 5 145 clocks = <b>85.8 us</b>

  total = (n1-1)*(10 + (n2-1)*(4*n3+7)) + 4

  <b>18 ms = 1.08 million clocks</b> - the longest single
  timed interval in the project, 6.4x the WS2812
  reset and 900x the WS2812 bit cell. This is what
  forces a three-level delay routine: as NOPs it
  would not fit in 1 024 words of instruction memory.
end note
note over WIRE
  <b>18 093.0 us of LOW</b>   (required: > 18 000 us)

  0.5 % long, and that is the RIGHT DIRECTION for a
  minimum: a start signal that is too short is a reset
  the sensor ignores.
end note
SENSOR -> WIRE : the sensor sees the reset

== 2. the host's high window: a driven high, then a RELEASE ==

CPU -> PAD : TXPIN = DHT_DATA - pull HIGH
note over CPU
  FINE pair (n2,n3) = (2,6), n1 = 45
  one outer step = 41 clocks = <b>0.68 us</b>
end note
note over WIRE
  <b>30.0 us of HIGH</b>   (band: 20 .. 40 us)

  This middle step is NOT OPTIONAL. The sensor pulls
  its own line low to answer, so the host cannot just
  stop driving and let the pull-up win - the sensor
  would see a line that never went high and would not
  start converting. Pulling it high for 30 us
  guarantees a clean high-to-low transition to answer
  with, and only then does the host become a reader.
end note
CPU -> PAD : PINOE = 0x00 - RELEASE
note over PAD
  <b>THE TB MEASURES THE RELEASE ON pin_oe, NOT ON THE LINE.</b>

  The host drives high and then releases, and the
  pull-up holds the line high throughout, so on the
  WIRE the release moves nothing at all. A TB that
  looks for a falling edge here waits forever or, if
  it is looking for the wrong thing, measures the
  window from a transition that is not one.
end note
note over WIRE
  the line does NOT fall on the release -
  the pull-up already holds it high
end note

== 3. the acknowledge: 80 low, 80 high, NEVER TIMED ==

SENSOR -> WIRE : pull LOW for 80 us
note over SENSOR
  <b>80 us LOW</b>
end note
SENSOR -> WIRE : release, 80 us HIGH
note over SENSOR
  <b>80 us HIGH</b>
end note
note over CPU
  The firmware does not time this. It falls into the
  SAME edge-wait loop it uses for bits: the first round
  waits out the acknowledge, the second one is bit 0.

  Nothing on the wire announces when the acknowledge is
  over, so timing it would be another delay constant to
  fit and another place to be wrong. One loop, two uses.
end note

== 4. bit 0: the value IS the width ==

group a 0-bit - 50 us low, 28 us release
  SENSOR -> WIRE : pull LOW
  note over WIRE
    <b>50 us of LOW</b> - the bit's prefix. Every bit
    has one, so the host cannot use it as the value.
  end note
  CPU -> SMP : w_lo: spin until the line is LOW
  note over CPU
    a spin loop on the pin, polling every three
    instructions, so an edge is caught within 50 ns
    against a 26 us window - four thousand times finer
    than anything here needs.
  end note
  SENSOR -> WIRE : RELEASE
  CPU -> SMP : w_hi: spin until the line is HIGH
  note over WIRE
    <b>28 us of HIGH</b> - the SHORT end of the
    datasheet's 26-28 us window. A sensor inside its
    own specification, and the TB drives this on
    purpose so the margin is measured against the
    worst legal sensor rather than the typical one.
  end note
  CPU -> PAD : FINE delay, n1 = 67 = 45 us
  SMP -> WIRE : sample
  note over SMP
    <b>THE SAMPLE: 17.0 us of margin on the 0 side.</b>
    The 0's release has already ENDED and the next bit
    has pulled the line low again.

    A sample at 20 us would be INSIDE the release, where
    a 0 and a 1 are indistinguishable - the mutation
    case dh-sample-instant moves it to 14 us and the
    decode fails.
  end note
end group

group a 1-bit - 50 us low, 70 us release
  SENSOR -> WIRE : pull LOW, then RELEASE
  note over WIRE
    <b>50 us LOW</b> then <b>70 us HIGH</b>
  end note
  CPU -> SMP : the same two waits, then 45 us
  SMP -> WIRE : sample
  note over SMP
    <b>25.0 us of margin on the 1 side.</b>
    The 1's release is still running, so the line is
    still high.
  end note
end group

note over CPU, SENSOR
  ... 40 bits. 24 of the 39 bit-transitions are real,
  which is the TB's non-vacuity floor: a frame of
  all-0s or all-1s would make a width-based decoder
  trivially correct and the check would prove nothing.
  ...
  <b>Bytes: 2c 01 aa 55 2c</b>   (humidity int, humidity dec,
  temperature int, temperature dec, checksum)
  and 2c + 01 + aa + 55 = 0x12C, and 0x12C & 0xFF = <b>0x2C</b>
  ...
  then a <b>50 us bus hold</b> after the fortieth bit, the line released,
  the sensor left alone, and the firmware parks with dmem[15] = 1.
end note

== the timing table, and what each window protects ==

note over CPU, SENSOR
  |= what |= required |= produced | which side | what it protects |=
  | start signal | > 18 000 us | <b>18 093.0 us</b> | host | a reset the sensor ignores |
  | host high window | 20 .. 40 us | <b>30.0 us</b> | host | a clean high-to-low for the sensor to answer |
  | the release | - | measured on <b>pin_oe</b> | host | nothing to see on the line: the pull-up already holds it high |
  | acknowledge | 80 / 80 us | <b>not timed</b> | sensor | skipped by the edge loop, not fitted |
  | a 0's release | 26 .. 28 us | 28 us driven | sensor | the worst legal sensor, so the margin is real |
  | a 1's release | ~70 us | 70 us driven | sensor | ditto |
  | the sample | inside the gap | <b>+45 us</b> | host | <b>17.0 us</b> past the 0, <b>25.0 us</b> before the 1 ends |
  ...
  <b>Why the 45 us is AIMED and the other numbers are only SATISFIED.</b> The start signal
  and the host window have bands, and landing anywhere inside them is correct. The
  sample has no band: it has to be after the 0's release ENDS and before the 1's
  release ENDS, because inside the release the line is high for both values. That is
  the one number in this program that has a target rather than a range, and it is the
  FINE pair that makes it aimable - with the coarse (6,255) step, 20.7 us would be
  inside the host window and 34 us would be outside it, so neither could be aimed at
  anything.
  ...
  <b>Why the margins are asymmetric, and why that is the sensor's doing.</b> 17 us on the
  0 side, 25 us on the 1 side. The 0's release is 26-28 us and the 1's is ~70 us, so
  a sample aimed at the MIDDLE of the gap would sit at 49 us - 21 us of margin on the
  0 side and 21 on the 1. 45 us trades the 0's margin for the 1's, which is the right
  trade: the 0's window is 14x tighter than the sample's precision, and the 1's is not.
end note

@enduml
